295:The current account deficit is, by definition, matched by offsetting transactions in the financial and capital accounts, with any difference in the recorded flows listed as a statistical discrepancy. In the first quarter of 2000, recorded inflows for foreign as sets in the United States exceeded out flows for U.S. as sets abroad by $286.9 billion at an annual rate. A jump in net foreign purchases of U.S. securities, particularly equities, led the increase.
